course: everyday
lesson: 315
title: Peer pressure, happy secrets and being trusted
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Friends sometimes dare or push you to do something gross, unsafe or against the rules. You never have to prove anything: «No thanks» and changing the subject is enough.
explain: Some secrets are happy ones, like a Christmas present for Mum. Keeping quiet and acting surprised lets the giver enjoy the moment.
explain: Trust is like a glass vase: quick to break and slow to mend. If you have lied before, the only way to win trust back is to keep telling the truth, day after day.
explain: Being trustworthy means behaving well when no one is watching, and taking care when someone small depends on you.
fact: You do not have to accept a dare; your health matters more than proving anything.
fact: Saying «No thanks» and changing the subject is a good way to turn down a dare.
fact: Skipping a lesson gets you in trouble and worries school staff, who must always know where you are.
fact: A happy secret, like a surprise present, should be kept, because spoiling it takes the joy from the giver.
fact: Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild.
fact: After a lie, parents may check up on you more; telling the truth again and again lets trust grow back slowly.
fact: Being good when no one is watching is what makes you trustworthy.
fact: A private, harmless secret about a friend can be kept, but a secret that hides harm or danger must be told to a trusted adult.
fact: A grown-up should never ask a child to keep secrets from their parents; if one does, tell your mum, dad or another trusted adult.
fact: Asking someone about their boyfriend or girlfriend as a dare is rude; people's private lives are not a joke.
fact: When you look after a younger child, keep them in sight and call an adult if anything happens.
exercise: Friends dare you to eat a worm from the garden. What is a good answer? | «No thanks», then change the subject | Eat it quickly so they stop asking | Dare them to eat two instead | Pretend to eat it and hide it
exercise: Why don't you have to accept a dare to eat something unsafe? | You have nothing to prove, and your health matters more | Dares only count on your birthday | Friends always forget dares by the next day, so it doesn't really count | You can accept it if nobody films it
exercise: A friend says, «Let's hide in the toilets and skip music, nobody will notice.» What should you do? | Stay in class | Go with her, just this once | Skip it but come back for the next lesson | Tell her to go and you will cover for her
exercise: Why is skipping a lesson more serious than it seems? | It gets you in trouble and staff worry because they must know where you are | Music lessons do not matter anyway, because nothing in music is ever tested | Teachers never count who is in class | It is only serious if you miss a test
exercise: You find the bike your parents have hidden as your brother's Christmas present. What should you do? | Keep quiet and let him be surprised | Tell your brother straight away | Tell your friends so they can tell him | Ride it once before Christmas
exercise: Your dad is planning a surprise party for Mum and she asks why he is whispering. What is kind? | Keep the surprise and act as if nothing is going on | Tell her about the party at once | Tell her a long scary story to distract her from the whispering | Tell her Dad is in trouble
exercise: Last month a boy lied about finishing his homework. Now his parents check it every night. What will win back their trust? | Telling the truth again and again over time | Promising very loudly, again and again, that he never lies | Being angry that they check | Hiding his homework so they can't check
exercise: Why does trust take longer to rebuild than to lose? | One lie can break it, but it takes many honest acts to show you have changed | Parents always forget lies quickly | Trust can never be won back at all | It only takes one proper apology to fix it all, as long as you really mean it
exercise: Your teacher leaves the room for a few minutes. How should you behave? | As if the teacher were still there | Have fun because nobody is watching | Look through the teacher's desk | Sit quietly but copy a friend's work
exercise: What makes a person truly trustworthy? | Being good even when no one is watching | Being good only when a grown-up can see you | Saying «trust me» very often | Never being asked to do anything
exercise: Your mum asks you to watch your baby brother in the garden while she gets the washing. What should you do? | Keep him in sight and call her if anything happens | Go inside to play a game for a minute, since he's happy on the grass | Leave him if he seems happy | Play a game on your phone while he plays
exercise: Why does looking after a younger sibling matter so much, even for a few minutes? | Someone small depends on you | Babies can always look after themselves | Nothing can happen in a few minutes | It is only important at night
test: At a sleepover, the older kids say you are a baby unless you drink a cup of washing-up liquid. What is TRUE? | Refusing is right, because your health matters more than a dare | You must do it, or the older kids will say you are not a real friend | A small sip is safe for anyone | It is fine if everyone else does it
test: A girl in Glasgow skips PE to sit in the library without telling anyone. Why might the staff panic? | They must always know where every child is | They want her to do more running and get fitter | The library is closed in the afternoon | Skipping PE is never noticed
test: Your grandma tells you she has knitted your sister a scarf for her birthday. Your sister asks what she's getting. What is best? | Keep the surprise and say «You'll have to wait and see!» | Tell her straight away, because keeping secrets from your sister is always bad | Tell her and ask her to act surprised | Make up a scary lie about the present
test: Which is a happy secret that is fine to keep? | A surprise present for a friend's birthday | An adult asking you to hide that they hurt you | A friend who is being bullied every day | Someone who took money from your class
test: A boy who once lied about where he went now always tells the truth. What will most likely happen? | His parents will slowly trust him again | They will trust him fully the very next day | They will never trust him again | They will stop caring where he goes
test: The supply teacher in Melbourne steps out and the class starts throwing paper. What shows you are trustworthy? | Carrying on with your work as if she were there | Throwing paper too, so you fit in and nobody thinks you're a goody | Hiding so you can't be blamed | Writing names of others to tell on everyone
test: You are minding your little cousin at the playground and she climbs too high on the frame. What should you do? | Stay close and call an adult for help | Look away and hope she gets down | Go home to fetch a game | Tell her to jump down quickly
test: A friend says, «If you won't jump off the garage roof, you're chicken.» What is the smartest answer? | «No thanks, I'm not doing that», and change the subject | Jump, so he stops calling you names in front of everyone | Ask him to jump first | Jump from halfway up instead
test: they dared me to skip maths n hide in the loos | Say no thanks, staff must always know where you are | Do it once, nobody will notice that one kid is missing | Do it if you're back by lunch | Only skip the boring half
test: lied to mum last week, now she checks on me loads. unfair | Keep telling the truth and trust grows back slowly | It is unfair, she should just forget all about it by now | Lie better next time | Ask her to stop checking right now
test: dad's surprise party is sat, he's asking what we're doing | Keep it secret, it's a happy surprise | Tell him, secrets are always bad | Tell him if he asks twice | Give him a big hint
test: watching my cousin at the playground, mates want me on the other side | Keep him in sight and call an adult if needed | Go, he'll be fine on his own for a bit | Leave him with a stranger | Tell him to stay by the gate and not move till you're back
explain: A boy in New Zealand took a dare to hold his breath underwater as long as he could; he blacked out and a lifeguard had to pull him out.
explain: A girl in Florida skipped a lesson to hang out behind the gym; the school could not find her, parents were called, and police were about to search.
fact: Holding your breath underwater too long can make you black out without warning, and many people drown that way.
fact: When a child is missing at school, staff must treat it as an emergency until the child is found.
test: they dared me to stay underwater the longest | Say no; you can black out and drown | Do it, just come up when dizzy | Do it if a friend is watching | Do it in the shallow end
test: wanna skip maths and hide in the toilets | Stay in class; staff will think you're missing | Do it, nobody ever counts who's in maths anyway | Skip it if a mate comes too | Skip it and say you were ill
test: looking after my little cousin at the park, got a text | Keep your eyes on her first | Reply quickly, she'll be fine | Let her go off alone | Sit and chat on your phone
test: did u hear?? some boys had a contest who could hold their breath longest underwater and one "just went limp, the lifeguard pulled him out". lesson? | Never do breath-holding contests underwater; you can black out | Breath holding is fine if you're a good swimmer and come up when it hurts | Only hold it for a minute | It's safe in shallow water
test: my sister says a kid skipped maths to hang out behind the gym and "the whole school was searched, police and everything". what's the true bit | Staff must treat a missing child as an emergency, so never skip class | Skip it, no one will ever notice one kid gone | Police come for every late child | Skipping is fine if you come back
test: kids say Amy "spilled the surprise party, she's a blabbermouth and always ruins everything". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; just keep happy secrets yourself | Yes, she ruins everything | Yes, and don't invite her | Yes, and tell the birthday girl
test: my cousin said "I'll tell u a secret if u swear: I kissed someone at the party, don't tell auntie". what kind of secret is it? | A private, harmless one about her; keep it unless she's in danger | A dangerous one, so tell the whole class straight away | A funny one, post it online | A fake one, so tell auntie anyway
test: older kids dare you: "go ask the dinner lady if she's got a boyfriend lol" — what's right? | Say no; it's rude and her private life is not a joke | Do it, a dare is a dare | Ask her husband instead | Write it on a note and leave it
test: a grown-up you know says "our special friendship is our secret, don't tell your mum". what do you do? | Tell your mum or another trusted adult; grown-ups should not ask kids for secrets like that | Keep it, he is being nice to you and you don't want to get him into any trouble | Ask him to buy you something to keep it | Promise and forget about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate response to a dare? | It may be refused, since health matters more than proving anything | It must be accepted, in order that one retain the respect of one's friends | It may be refused only by younger children | It must be performed if repeated twice
exam: Identify the principal danger of holding one's breath underwater for too long. | One may black out without warning and drown | One's ears may become blocked | One may swallow too much water | One may become cold
exam: It is given that a child's trust has been damaged by a lie. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Repeated truthfulness allows trust to grow back slowly | A single sincere apology restores trust at once, completely | Trust cannot be regained in any case | Trust returns after a fixed time
exam: State the reason for which a happy secret, such as a surprise present, should be kept. | Spoiling it takes the joy from the giver | Secrets must always be kept without exception | The giver would otherwise demand payment | It is required by law
